<problem><title>Video review questions</title>
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title>Experiment with applet...</title>
<p>Use the <ref>applet</ref> on the following page to model the dynamical system:
  <mdn>
    <mrow name="diffEq">\frac{dI}{dt} \amp= \alpha I (1 - I) - \mu I</mrow>
    <mrow>\text{where,}\;\;\alpha \amp= 1</mrow>
    <mrow>\mu \amp= 0.2</mrow>
  </mdn>
You can adjust the value for <m>\alpha</m> with the red slider  and the value for <m>\mu</m> with the purple slider.</p>
  <figure suppressFigureNameInCaption="true">    
    <image source="doenet:8KgAFquisWW4t8oA8i4CBe" asfilename="SIS applet howto 1.png" size="small" aspectRatio="830/374" mimeType="image/png" displayMode="inline" imageName="graphic showing sliders in applet" authorName="Virginia Mae" licenseCodes="CC-BY"><shortDescription>graphic showing sliders in applet</shortDescription></image>
    
    <caption>Adjust sliders to match given <m>\alpha</m> and <m>\mu</m> values.</caption>
  </figure>

  <p>For this model, since 
    <answer>
      <choice>the recovery rate, <m>\mu</m>, is greater than the infection rate, <m>\alpha</m></choice>
      <choice credit="1">the infection rate, <m>\alpha</m>, is greater than the recovery rate, <m>\mu</m></choice>
    </answer>, there are 
    <answer inline="true">
      <choice>1</choice>
      <choice credit="1">2</choice>
      <choice>3</choice>
    </answer> biologically plausible equilibria. 
  </p>
<p>Click on the checkboxes to show the "vector field" and the "equilibria". Next adjust the position of the initial condition (the point labeled <m>x_0</m> in the applet.)</p>
<p>Finally, click on the play button in the lower left corner of the graph to view an animation of the system.</p>
</exercise>
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title>Using the same applet...</title>
<p>Based on what you learned in the lesson on <ref to="doenet:7Z7pd9dxbBmFSwFMxsF35H">The stability of equilibria of a differential equation (video lesson)</ref>, use the <ref>applet</ref> to answer the following questions for the differential equation from <ref to="$diffEq">question 1</ref>.</p>
  
<hint><p>The applet displays the graph of the differential equation, <m>\frac{dI}{dt}</m>, in the left window and the <m>I</m> v. <m>t</m> graph in the right window.</p></hint>
<p>For the equilibrium at <m>I^* = 0</m>, the system is 
  <answer inline="true">
    <choice>stable</choice>
    <choice credit="1">unstable</choice>
  </answer>, as all solutions near the equilibrium point move away from the equilibrium. The other equilibrium is located at <m>I^* = </m> <answer>0.8</answer>, and it is 
  <answer inline="true">
    <choice credit="1">stable</choice>
    <choice>unstable</choice>
  </answer>. This is an unfortunate situation, because it means that as time passes, the population of infected and susceptible individuals will stabilize with the following proportions:
  <answer>
    <choice credit="1">80% infected and 20% susceptible</choice>
    <choice>20% infected and 80% susceptible</choice>
    <choice>50% infected and 50% susceptible</choice>
  </answer>.</p>
<hint><title>What should it look like?</title>
  <p>Using the applet, you should get something that looks like this for the graph of the differential equation (the graph on the left)</p>  
  
  <image source="doenet:oLSMFyaWjMMGwpnmAKjtbk" asfilename="diffeq graph.png" mimeType="image/png" imageName="Diff eq graph" authorName="Virginia Mae" licenseCodes="CC-BY"><shortDescription>graph of the differential equation</shortDescription></image>
<p>...and something that looks like this for the <m>I</m> v. <m>t</m> graph (the graph on the right).</p>
  <image source="doenet:xobmcY2dxNAAALx51TqN9h" asfilename="I v t graph.png" aspectRatio="956/1248" mimeType="image/png" imageName="I v. t graph" authorName="Virginia Mae" licenseCodes="CC-BY"><shortDescription>I v t graph</shortDescription></image>
</hint>
</exercise>

</problem>



